You hold your ground for a long, slow breath, shield forward, the lantern steady, and wait for whatever dragged that iron to come. The dark gives back only the drip, and the soft settling of the stone, and the draught at your face. Nothing comes.

So you call again, soft as a prayer at vespers: that Prior Hessa has sent you, that the Anvil Father's house has not forgotten them, that you have come to bring them home.

For a long moment there is nothing. Then, far down in the crack's throat, a pale light flutters to life — small and cold, the color of that fading dust on the drift floor — trembles once, and is gone. And threaded out of the same dark comes a word, thin as a drawn iron wire, too far and small to be certain of. It might have been "cold." It might have been "come." Then silence, and only the steady whisper of the draught.
