You keep your shield up and answer, low into the crack: "I hear you. Hold fast, I'm coming."

You raise the lantern to the opening and look through. The mouth is no wider than a man's shoulders, its edges fused to black glass where the lightning licked out of it. Not far in, though, the rock opens into a natural passage cut by old water — rough and streaming downward, the glassy scorch running along it like veins of black glass. Out of it rises the steady, cold draught, and it smells again of scorched dust and of that keen air before a storm.

Your mail is broad and your shield broader; you will not walk in frontways. So you turn sidelong, draw the shield tight along your hip, and ease the lantern through the gap ahead of you. Your twine is already fixed to the piton in the crack's jaw — you set a hand on it once, to be certain, then work yourself after the lantern. The rock rasps along the rings of your mail, shoulder and hip, and then, after a few feet, the passage widens enough to draw a full breath.

Within, the floor falls away in worn, water-slick steps of stone. Your lantern-light runs down them and is swallowed, and the draught moves steady as a river against your face. Your sword-hand stays free, the steel only a breath from your grip.


