You test each worn step before you trust it, your boot finding the stone by turns slick and solid, and go down into that long, river-cut throat, shield up and lantern low.

As you reach to pay out twine behind you, your fingers close on nothing. The twine is spent — tied with the piton back at the crack's jaw. Your rope, too, lies far behind you, strung from the mine-mouth timber down the first of the drift; you paid it all out coming in. So you have no line to lay here, only the twine glimmering behind you at the fissure and the one passage going down: one way in, one way back, if the stone will keep its word.

You descend, watching. The glassy scorch-veins run on with you, branching down the walls, and here and there the black glass is thickest where the lightning lingered, as though the light itself moved down this way and burned as it went.

And then, low on the rock at the edge of the lantern glow, you catch it: faint smears of that cold luminous dust, like a trail of dying starlight, going down ahead of you, paling and brightening again the deeper the passage runs.

Far below, at the very limit of what your light can reach, a pale light breathes once — and holds.


