You shutter the lantern down to a narrow blade of light and go on, slow, shield up. The passage falls into true dark, and your dwarf-sight takes up the slack, washing the walls to gray; the pale light below stands out all the sharper now that your own flame is dimmed — a small cold point that does not move. Whether it is a flame, a face, or something walking, you cannot say: the dark and the distance keep its edges from you, and your eyes can give you no more than that.

So you call, soft: "Borrin? Alda? Kell?"

The light does not waver. For a long moment only the drip answers, and the slow rush of the draught.

Then, threaded up out of the dark from just that same direction, the thin voice comes again — closer now, and no longer uncertain. It says one word, clear as a wire drawn through cold stone: "Come."

And behind it, once, faint: the drag of iron over rock.


