You ease down a few more steps, the narrow beam cutting one pale track through the dark, your sword-hand loose on the hilt.

For a long moment the voice is silent. Then, thin and small, it comes again, and this time it gives the name — but slowly, like a man dragging the word up out of deep cold water, uncertain of his own tongue: "Kell."

There is no relief in it. The word might be an answer, or a guess, or something trying the name on to see how it fits.

Then the iron scrapes again. You listen hard for whether it is nearer and cannot say. The close stone takes the sound and throws it back off the walls, so it seems to come from no single place; the draught gusts, and the pale light below shudders once, as if you had spoken across the mouth of a guttering candle.


