You hold still on the step and put the question low. For a little while there is only the drip.

Then the thin voice finds the words, and each comes slow and spent, as though the man had to haul it up from far down.

"Alda's here... beside me. She's cold, and she won't wake." A breath. "I can't walk. The cold's got into my legs. Into my hands." A longer pause. "Borrin went after it — after the light. It was walking, and he followed it down. It's in him now. He's gone all lit up inside."

And as he speaks, your eyes at last find the shapes in the dark below.

The passage opens into a low pocket of stone, and two men are in it. One sits slumped against the wall, knees drawn up, arms wound about himself — that is the voice. His face is a pale blur in the glow. The other lies stretched on his back beside him, one arm flung out, and does not move at all.

And a little way apart, standing as still as stone, is the light.

It is not a flame. It is tall and thin, man-shaped, and it makes no sound. Its feet are narrow and without heels, set close together; its long arms hang at its sides; and where a face should be the glow runs brightest but empty, featureless, the cold white of the dust you have followed all this way.

It is watching you.


>>>>>> round 15, the player: I keep my shield up and slowly open the lantern shutter to full light, stepping down to place myself between the light-thing and the two men. In a steady voice I say, "Kell, stay still and keep talking to me." I keep my sword hand ready, ready to draw if it moves, and I mutter a quick prayer to the Anvil Father as I watch the thing's empty face.

