Your sword comes down in a clean arc — and the light is not where the blade is. It sways aside like a flame in a draught, and you cut only cold air.

In the same breath it is on you. There is no weight to it, no striking limb, but one long, narrow hand reaches out and brushes the iron over your arm, and the cold of that touch goes through mail and leather and wool at once, dry and deep. Your breath catches in your throat and comes out fogging white. The shape draws back a little, its featureless face tilted, and the lantern-light makes its edges shimmer like heat over a forge — only cold, cold that means to get in.

Your arm stings and numbs where it touched, but you are still on your feet, sword up, and the thing hangs in the air before you, waiting on you.
