Your second stroke comes down and again the thing is not there to meet it — it blurs sideways, and your blade whistles through the space where its chest should be.

Then it is on you once more, and this time the touch is no glancing brush. The long pale hand closes over your sword-arm at the wrist, and the cold pours in through the mail like iron left all night in the snow. Your arm goes dull and white at the edges in an instant, and the breath is dragged out of you in a cloud that hangs in the air. For one bad moment you cannot feel your own fingers on the hilt.

A sound threads up from behind you — Kell, half a sob, half a word: "Don't let it hold you — it drinks the warmth out."

The shape lets go and draws back an inch, that smooth, featureless face still set full upon you, and the lantern-light shudders across its edges, which waver like the lip of a cold flame.

Your arm hangs heavy and numbed, but your grip holds. The thing floats just within a sword's length, waiting on you.

The reply has already been delivered to the player. My turn is complete — Brakka is at 5/14 HP, and it is now Round 3, Brakka's turn.


