The warmth of the Anvil Father answers you at once. It kindles in your chest and rolls out along your arms, and the fog of your own breath thins; feeling comes back into your sword-hand in a hot prickling rush, as if you had thrust it deep into a forge-glow. You are yourself again.

You plant your feet across the two miners and swing. But the blade shears only air — the thing leans away by no more than the width of a hand, unhurried, and your cut does not so much as stir its light.

Once more the cold hand comes for you, and though it only grazes your forearm this time, the touch clenches your muscles into a spasm. You flinch, your grip tightening against your will, and the cold sinks a little deeper before the ember in your chest pushes it back out.

Kell's voice rises behind you, thin and urgent: "It's not tiring. It never tires."

The thing hangs before you, silent, that bright empty face tilted as if considering the worth of what stands in its way.


