You shift your stance and put your shoulder behind the cut, but the blade comes down through nothing a fourth time — the shape sways out from under the steel as if the very light of it curves away, and your arm jars against empty air.

Then it sweeps at you with that long pale hand, and this time you catch it full on the rim of your shield. There is a brittle crackle, and frost blooms white across the iron in a running web, but the touch cannot get through to you. The cold glances off and you are unharmed.

Kell's answer comes ragged out of the dark at your back, an old terror trembling through it. "It didn't fight Borrin. He never cried out, never ran. I was spent, and Alda was already down, and Borrin just… stood up out of the dark and walked to it, slow, smiling, like he was going home to his own bed. He put his hand up, and it poured into him. Through his mouth, through his eyes. It lit him up from the inside until he stopped being Borrin."

A long, shaking breath. "Fire — our lamps. It kept its distance while the flame was high. When my lamp burned down to a stub, that's when it came in close. It wants the dark. The dark, and the cold."


